From: Codex Executor <codex-executor@local>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 02:26:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 21/22] sheet route: block with a bonded skin, the coupling
 contract proven end to end

Every earlier assembly figure was measured on internal interfaces, where both sides use the same carrier by
construction, so agreement there was not evidence for the skin coupling. This adds the external arm.

sheet_skin_validation.py builds a 2x1x1 cut block with a Tet10 slab extruded from the cut plane and bonded to it, and
solves three models of the same geometry with the same block mesh, the same skin mesh and the same loads, differing
only in the space the joint traction lives in: the block's fine cut-cap nodes follow the skin's own P1 field (REF),
the carrier P1 field whose nodes are the skin's own nodes (CON), or nothing because the cells are their Schur
operators (ASM). Midpoint refinement keeps the carrier nodes an exact subset of the skin's joint nodes, so a carrier
node and its skin node are the same unknown and neither body is coarsened in CON.

Contract cost, CON vs REF: 0.36% (pull), 0.51% (shear), 0.47% (bending), 2.34% (16H), 4.49% (8H), 3.34% (4H) at the
production preset, half of that at the fast preset. Positive throughout, as a Rayleigh-Ritz restriction must be.
Assembly error, ASM vs CON: 1.9e-4 to 1.8e-3, with a skin attached and 1709 of the 2721 joint carrier nodes carrying
no cell stiffness at all. Skin outer-face displacement: CON vs REF 0.34% to 3.1%, ASM vs CON 2.2e-4 to 3.0e-3.

The side arm --skin-on-carrier meshes the skin itself on the carrier: 1.4% to 3.8% too stiff, so the delivered joint
mesh should be midpoint-refined for the skin's own accuracy, which keeps the carrier nodes a subset and the coupling
exact. An earlier version of the script tied the skin's joint nodes to the carrier and so coarsened the skin, which
reported a 21% to 42% "contract cost" that was almost entirely the skin's under-resolution; the arms now separate the
two.

Not settled: the cost roughly doubles between the two presets and the reference itself moves 1.2%, so these are lower
bounds pending a third resolution; and the skin is an isotropic slab of the cells' modulus, not a real laminate.

